= Solution
{parent=a}

Put $r=g_1=\operatorname{diag}(\alpha,\alpha^2)$ and $s=g_2$, using $\alpha^*=\alpha^{-1}=\alpha^2$. Then
$$
r^3=s^2=I_2,\qquad srs=r^{-1}.
$$
Every word can therefore be put in the form $r^k$ or $r^ks$, with $k=0,1,2$. These give six distinct <matrices>:
$$
\boxed{\begin{aligned}
G=\bigg\{&\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\0&1\end{pmatrix},
\begin{pmatrix}\alpha&0\\0&\alpha^2\end{pmatrix},
\begin{pmatrix}\alpha^2&0\\0&\alpha\end{pmatrix},\\
&\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\1&0\end{pmatrix},
\begin{pmatrix}0&\alpha\\\alpha^2&0\end{pmatrix},
\begin{pmatrix}0&\alpha^2\\\alpha&0\end{pmatrix}\bigg\}.
\end{aligned}}
$$
The <group isomorphism> $r\mapsto(123)$, $s\mapsto(12)$ identifies this <group> with the <symmetric group> $S_3$, with <permutations> composed from right to left.

Conjugation by $s$ exchanges $r,r^2$. Conjugation by $r$ cycles $s,rs,r^2s$, because $r(r^ks)r^{-1}=r^{k+2}s$. Their <conjugacy classes> are
$$
\boxed{\{I_2\},\qquad\{r,r^2\},\qquad\{s,rs,r^2s\},}
$$
with sizes $1,2,3$. There is no mixing between them: their elements have orders one, three and two, respectively.

= b
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The <matrix> $r$ has distinct <eigenvalues> $\alpha,\alpha^2$. Any one-dimensional <invariant subspace> for the defining <representation> would therefore have to be one of its two coordinate <eigenspaces>. But $s$ exchanges those lines, so neither is invariant under the whole <group>. Hence $R_2$ is an <irreducible representation> over the <complex numbers>.

There are three complex <irreducible representations> up to <equivalence of group representations>, because there are three <conjugacy classes>. The <trivial representation> and the <sign representation> already give two inequivalent <one-dimensional representations>. Together with $R_2$, their <dimensions> satisfy the <sum of squares of irreducible representation dimensions>:
$$
\boxed{1^2+1^2+2^2=6=|G|,\qquad\text{dimensions }1,1,2.}
$$
Thus there are no further <irreducible representations>. Alternatively the <character> of $R_2$ is $(2,-1,0)$, and its <character inner product> with itself is $(4+2)/6=1$, independently confirming irreducibility.

= c
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

The <irreducible representations> can all be specified on the generators:
$$
\boxed{\begin{array}{c|cc}
&R(r)&R(s)\\\hline
\mathbf1&1&1\\
\varepsilon&1&-1\\
R_2&\begin{pmatrix}\alpha&0\\0&\alpha^2\end{pmatrix}&\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\1&0\end{pmatrix}
\end{array}}
$$
The first is the <trivial representation> and the second is the <sign representation>. To see directly that these exhaust the <one-dimensional representations>, their scalar images commute, so the relation $srs=r^{-1}$ forces $r=r^{-1}$. Combining $r^2=1$ with $r^3=1$ gives $r=1$, and then $s$ may be either sign.

Taking <traces> gives the <character table>, in the <conjugacy class> order of <2/a/solution>:
$$
\boxed{\begin{array}{c|rrr}
&I_2&r&s\\\hline
\text{class size}&1&2&3\\\hline
\mathbf1&1&1&1\\
\varepsilon&1&1&-1\\
R_2&2&-1&0
\end{array}}
$$
Here $\alpha+\alpha^2=-1$ gives the middle entry in the last row. The weighted <character inner products> of distinct rows vanish, and the norm of each row is one. Together with <2/b/solution>, this proves completeness of the table.

= d
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

Let $S=s=\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\1&0\end{pmatrix}$. Entrywise <complex conjugation> interchanges the diagonal entries of $r$ and fixes $s$, so
$$
SR_2(r)^*S^{-1}=R_2(r),\qquad SR_2(s)^*S^{-1}=R_2(s).
$$
Since $r,s$ generate the <group>, these identities hold for every element. Thus $S$ is an invertible <intertwiner>, proving
$$
\boxed{R_2^*\cong R_2.}
$$
The <character> of a <tensor product representation> is the product of the factor <characters>. Hence $\chi_{R_2\otimes R_2}=(4,1,0)$. Its <character inner products> with the three rows of <2/c/solution> give multiplicities $(1,1,1)$, so
$$
\boxed{R_2\otimes R_2\cong\mathbf1\oplus\varepsilon\oplus R_2.}
$$
For explicit <Clebsch-Gordan decomposition of group representations> <bases>, let $e_+,e_-$ be the coordinate <basis>. The <symmetric tensor> $e_+\otimes e_-+e_-\otimes e_+$ spans $\mathbf1$; the <antisymmetric tensor> $e_+\otimes e_--e_-\otimes e_+$ spans $\varepsilon$; and the ordered pair $e_-\otimes e_-,e_+\otimes e_+$ carries $R_2$, since $r$ acts by $\operatorname{diag}(\alpha,\alpha^2)$ and $s$ exchanges them. In particular $S^2R_2=\mathbf1\oplus R_2$ and $\Lambda^2R_2=\varepsilon$.

Multiplying by the <sign representation> preserves the <character> of $R_2$, because its <character> is zero on all three reflections. Thus $\varepsilon\otimes R_2\cong R_2$, and associativity gives
$$
\begin{aligned}
R_2^{\otimes3}
&\cong(\mathbf1\oplus\varepsilon\oplus R_2)\otimes R_2\\
&\cong R_2\oplus R_2\oplus(\mathbf1\oplus\varepsilon\oplus R_2).
\end{aligned}
$$
Therefore
$$
\boxed{R_2\otimes R_2\otimes R_2\cong\mathbf1\oplus\varepsilon\oplus3R_2.}
$$
The <dimensions> give $8=1+1+3\cdot2$. Its two one-dimensional summands can also be seen directly: $e_+^{\otimes3}+e_-^{\otimes3}$ and $e_+^{\otimes3}-e_-^{\otimes3}$ transform as $\mathbf1$ and $\varepsilon$, respectively.

