= Solution
{parent=c}

Use upper indices for the defining <representation> of <SU(6)> and lower indices for its <dual representation>. The <Young tableaux> below display tensor-slot labels; their shapes determine the <Young diagrams>. Full columns of height six are <determinant representations> and can be removed for <SU(6)>.
$$
\begin{array}{c|c|c|c|c}
\text{representation}&\text{Dynkin labels}&\text{tableau}&\text{tensor}&\dim\\\hline
\mathbf6&(1,0,0,0,0)&\boxed{1}&v^a&6\\[3pt]
\overline{\mathbf6}&(0,0,0,0,1)&\begin{array}{l}\boxed{1}\\[-3pt]\boxed{2}\\[-3pt]\boxed{3}\\[-3pt]\boxed{4}\\[-3pt]\boxed{5}\end{array}&w_a&6\\[3pt]
\mathbf{21}&(2,0,0,0,0)&\boxed{1}\boxed{2}&S^{(ab)}&21\\[3pt]
\mathbf{15}&(0,1,0,0,0)&\begin{array}{l}\boxed{1}\\[-3pt]\boxed{2}\end{array}&A^{[ab]}&15\\[3pt]
\mathbf{35}&(1,0,0,0,1)&\begin{array}{l}\boxed{1}\boxed{2}\\[-3pt]\boxed{3}\\[-3pt]\boxed{4}\\[-3pt]\boxed{5}\\[-3pt]\boxed{6}\end{array}&T^a{}_b,\ T^a{}_a=0&35
\end{array}
$$
Here all indices run from one to six. The <highest weight> <Dynkin labels> follow from the differences of successive row lengths. In the same order the row lengths are $(1)$, $(1,1,1,1,1)$, $(2)$, $(1,1)$ and $(2,1,1,1,1)$.

The defining <vector> transforms as $v^a\mapsto U^a{}_bv^b$. For a <unitary representation>, the <conjugate representation> is equivalent to the <dual representation>, so $\overline{\mathbf6}$ can be written as a <covector> with $w_a\mapsto w_b(U^{-1})^b{}_a$. Its five-box column is equivalently a totally <antisymmetric tensor> $A^{a_1\cdots a_5}$, related by the invariant alternating <tensor> $w_a=\epsilon_{aa_1\cdots a_5}A^{a_1\cdots a_5}/5!$.

The <symmetric tensor> transforms as $S^{ab}\mapsto U^a{}_cU^b{}_dS^{cd}$, with $6(6+1)/2=21$ independent components. The <antisymmetric tensor> has the same transformation law but obeys $A^{ab}=-A^{ba}$, giving $6(6-1)/2=15$ independent components. For the <adjoint representation>, $T\mapsto UTU^{-1}$; the <trace> constraint removes one component from the $36$ entries of a mixed <tensor>, leaving $35$. Its six-box <Young diagram> is obtained by converting the five-index column to a lower index and selecting the traceless mixed <tensor>.

Thus the requested <dimensions>, in the order above, are
$$
\boxed{6,\quad6,\quad21,\quad15,\quad35.}
$$

= d
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

In this part the earlier variable $n$ specializes to six. A <group representation> of $U(1)\times SU(6)$ factors through the <quotient group> $U(6)$ exactly when the central <subgroup> $K$ in <3/b/solution> acts trivially. For an irreducible <SU(6)> <tensor> <representation> represented by a <Young diagram> with $b$ boxes, the scalar <matrix> $\zeta^{-1}I_6$ acts as $\zeta^{-b}$, where $\zeta=e^{2\pi i/6}$.

The prescribed <representation> $\widetilde R(z,U)=R(U)$ gives no compensating phase from the first factor. Its value on the generator of $K$ is therefore $R(\zeta^{-1}I_6)=\zeta^{-b}I$. The box counts in <3/c/solution> give
$$
\begin{array}{c|ccccc}
R&\mathbf6&\overline{\mathbf6}&\mathbf{21}&\mathbf{15}&\mathbf{35}\\\hline
b\pmod6&1&5&2&2&0\\
\widetilde R(\zeta,\zeta^{-1}I_6)&\zeta^{-1}I&\zeta I&\zeta^{-2}I&\zeta^{-2}I&I
\end{array}
$$
Consequently
$$
\boxed{\text{Among these prescribed lifts, only the adjoint }\mathbf{35}\text{ descends to }U(6).}
$$
Concretely, conjugation $T\mapsto WTW^{-1}$ defines it for every $W\in U(6)$, and the scalar phase cancels.

The qualification “prescribed lifts” matters. The defining <representation> of $U(6)$ certainly exists, but its pullback is $(z,U)\mapsto zU$, not $(z,U)\mapsto U$. For instance $(\zeta,\zeta^{-1}I_6)$ and $(1,I_6)$ represent the same quotient element, while the latter prescription assigns them different <matrices> in the defining <representation>. More generally, for an integer $q$, a lift $z^qR(U)$ descends precisely when
$$
\boxed{q\equiv b\pmod6.}
$$
This <descent criterion for representations of U(n)> explains how all the listed <SU(6)> <representations> can be extended after choosing a suitable first-factor <U(1) charge>, even though only one descends with that charge fixed to zero.

