= Solution
{parent=a}

For a differentiable curve $Q(t)\in SO(5)$ with $Q(0)=I_5$, differentiate $Q(t)^TQ(t)=I_5$. Its tangent <matrix> $X=Q'(0)$ is real and satisfies $X^T+X=0$. Conversely the <matrix exponential> of any real <antisymmetric matrix> is orthogonal, and $\det e^{tX}=e^{t\operatorname{tr}X}=1$ because $\operatorname{tr}X=0$. Hence the real <special orthogonal Lie algebra> is
$$
\boxed{\mathfrak{so}(5)=\{X\in M_5(\mathbb R):X^T=-X\},\qquad [X,Y]=XY-YX.}
$$
Let $E_{ab}$ denote a <matrix unit>. A simple real <basis> is
$$
\boxed{M_{ab}=E_{ab}-E_{ba},\qquad 1\leq a<b\leq5.}
$$
Each independent entry above the diagonal supplies one real parameter, giving $\binom52=10$ parameters. The <commutator> of two <basis> <matrices> is
$$
[M_{ab},M_{cd}]=\delta_{bc}M_{ad}-\delta_{ac}M_{bd}-\delta_{bd}M_{ac}+\delta_{ad}M_{bc},
$$
where $M_{ba}=-M_{ab}$ and $M_{aa}=0$.

A <Cartan subalgebra> is spanned by the commuting <matrices> $M_{12},M_{34}$, which generate independent rotations in two orthogonal planes. A generic $h=aM_{12}+bM_{34}$, with $ab(a^2-b^2)\ne0$, has distinct complex <eigenvalues> $ia,-ia,ib,-ib,0$. A real <antisymmetric matrix> commuting with it preserves those two planes and the remaining line; it is a multiple of the rotation in each plane and is zero on the line. Thus the commuting <Lie subalgebra> has <dimension> two and is maximal toral. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\dim_{\mathbb R}\mathfrak{so}(5)=10,\qquad\operatorname{rank}\mathfrak{so}(5)=2.}
$$

= b
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Use the convention $A_{ij}=\langle\alpha_i,\alpha_j^\vee\rangle$, so the rows of the supplied <Cartan matrix> are the <simple roots> in <Dynkin label> coordinates. Realize the <B2 root system> in an orthonormal plane by
$$
\alpha_1=e_1-e_2,\qquad\alpha_2=e_2,\qquad
\alpha_1^\vee=e_1-e_2,\qquad\alpha_2^\vee=2e_2.
$$
Thus the first <simple root> is long and the second is short. The <Dynkin diagram> has two nodes joined by a double bond, with its arrow pointing to the short node:
$$
\boxed{\underset{\alpha_1\ \mathrm{long}}{\circ}\;\Longrightarrow\;\underset{\alpha_2\ \mathrm{short}}{\circ}.}
$$
A Euclidean <weight> $w=xe_1+ye_2$ has <Dynkin labels>
$$
(a,b)=(x-y,2y),\qquad (x,y)=(a+b/2,b/2).
$$
In particular the <fundamental weights> are $\omega_1=e_1$ and $\omega_2=(e_1+e_2)/2$. This long-first ordering is essential: sources that put the short <simple root> first interchange the two <Dynkin labels>.

The <highest weight> $(0,1)$ is the <spinor representation>. Its Euclidean <weights> are $(\pm\tfrac12,\pm\tfrac12)$, whose <Dynkin labels> are
$$
\boxed{\mathbf4=(0,1):\quad(0,1),\ (1,-1),\ (-1,1),\ (0,-1).}
$$
A lowering chain subtracts $\alpha_2=(-1,2)$, then $\alpha_1=(2,-2)$, then $\alpha_2$ again, passing through these four <weights> in the displayed order. All <weight multiplicities> are one.

The <highest weight> $(1,0)$ is the defining <vector representation>, with Euclidean <weights> $e_1,e_2,0,-e_2,-e_1$. Thus
$$
\boxed{\mathbf5=(1,0):\quad(1,0),\ (-1,2),\ (0,0),\ (1,-2),\ (-1,0).}
$$
Here a lowering chain subtracts $\alpha_1,\alpha_2,\alpha_2,\alpha_1$. Each <weight multiplicity> is again one.

Finally $(0,2)$ is the <highest root> $e_1+e_2=\alpha_1+2\alpha_2$, giving the <adjoint representation>. Its nonzero <weights> are the eight <roots> $\pm e_1,\pm e_2,\pm(e_1-e_2),\pm(e_1+e_2)$. The <Cartan subalgebra> supplies zero with <weight multiplicity> two, so
$$
\boxed{\mathbf{10}=(0,2):\quad
\pm(1,0),\quad\pm(-1,2),\quad\pm(2,-2),\quad\pm(0,2),\quad(0,0)^{[2]}.}
$$
The <dimensions> are $4,5,10$. Independently the long-first <Weyl dimension formula> is
$$
\dim V(a,b)=\frac{(a+1)(b+1)(a+b+2)(2a+b+3)}6,
$$
which gives those same values. These are <Lie algebra> <representations>; the four-dimensional <spinor representation> integrates to the <Spin group> $\operatorname{Spin}(5)$, and does not descend to the <special orthogonal group> $SO(5)$. A concrete obstruction is a $2\pi$ rotation: it is the identity in $SO(5)$ but acts as $-I_4$ on the <spinor representation>, as verified in <4/d/solution>.

